Answer: A calling method is the method that calls another method. Example:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
DemoApp.greet("Pravaha");
}
```

Here, main() is the calling method because it calls greet().

Answer: A called method is the method that is invoked by another method. Example:

```java
public static String greet(String name) {
return "Hello " + name;
}
```

Here, greet() is the called method because main() invokes it.

Answer: A method definition is the complete declaration and body of a method. Example:

```java
public static String greet(String name) {
return "Hello " + name;
}
```

It contains the return type, method name, parameters, and method body.

Answer: Consider:

```java
class DemoApp {
public static String greet(String name) {
return "Hello " + name;
}
}
```

Method call:

```java
String greeting = DemoApp.greet("Pravaha");
System.out.println(greeting);
```

Execution: "Pravaha" is passed as an argument. The parameter name receives "Pravaha". "Hello " is concatenated with name. The method returns "Hello Pravaha". The returned string is stored in greeting. The value is printed.

Answer: A left-shift operation by one position can be used.

```java
class Demo {
public static int multiplyByTwo(int value) {
return value << 1;
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
int result = multiplyByTwo(10);
System.out.println(result);
}
}
```

Output: Left-shifting an integer once is equivalent to multiplying it by two.

Answer: A right shift by one position can be used. Example:

```java
public static int divideByTwo(int value) {
return value >> 1;
}
```

For positive integer values, shifting once towards the right produces the same result as integer division by two.

