Answer: ```java
class LogicalDemo {
public static boolean logicalAnd(boolean a, boolean b) {
return a && b;
}
public static boolean logicalOr(boolean a, boolean b) {
return a || b;
}
public static boolean logicalNot(boolean value) {
return !value;
}
}
```

logicalAnd() and logicalOr() accept two parameters because they use binary operators. logicalNot() accepts one parameter because ! is a unary operator.

Answer: Logical NOT (!) is a unary operator. A unary operator works on only one operand. Example:

```java
public static boolean logicalNot(boolean value) {
return !value;
}
```

Logical AND and OR require two operands, but logical NOT requires only one.

