Answer: ```java
class RelationalDemo {
public static boolean isGreater(int a, int b) {
return a > b;
}
public static boolean isEqual(int a, int b) {
return a == b;
}
}
class LogicalDemo {
public static boolean logicalAnd(boolean a, boolean b) {
return a && b;
}
public static boolean logicalNot(boolean value) {
return !value;
}
}
class MainDemo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
boolean greaterResult =
RelationalDemo.isGreater(20, 10);
boolean equalResult =
RelationalDemo.isEqual(20, 10);
boolean finalResult =
LogicalDemo.logicalAnd(greaterResult, equalResult);
System.out.println(greaterResult);
System.out.println(equalResult);
System.out.println(finalResult);
System.out.println(LogicalDemo.logicalNot(finalResult));
}
}
```

Output: true false false true Line-by-line explanation RelationalDemo contains relational operations. LogicalDemo contains logical operations. MainDemo contains only the execution logic. isGreater(20, 10) returns true. isEqual(20, 10) returns false. logicalAnd(true, false) returns false. logicalNot(false) returns true. The program demonstrates separation of concerns at the class level.
